				<ul style=""><li style=""><a href="https://www.theflavorbender.com/easy-homemade-chicken-ramen/#0-main-components-of-ramen" style="">Main components of ramen</a></li><li style=""><a href="https://www.theflavorbender.com/easy-homemade-chicken-ramen/#1-most-popular-types-of-ramen" style="">Most popular types of ramen</a></li><li style=""><a href="https://www.theflavorbender.com/easy-homemade-chicken-ramen/#2-why-this-recipe-works" style="">Why this recipe works</a></li><li style=""><a href="https://www.theflavorbender.com/easy-homemade-chicken-ramen/#3-ramen-ingredients" style="">Ramen ingredients</a></li><li style=""><a href="https://www.theflavorbender.com/easy-homemade-chicken-ramen/#7-how-to-make-the-best-chicken-ramen" style="">How to make the best chicken ramen</a></li><li style=""><a href="https://www.theflavorbender.com/easy-homemade-chicken-ramen/#13-ingredient-substitutions" style="">Ingredient substitutions</a></li><li style=""><a href="https://www.theflavorbender.com/easy-homemade-chicken-ramen/#14-my-best-recipe-tips-" style="">My best recipe tips</a></li></ul>
			</div>
		</div></div>


<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph" id="0-japanese-ramen-is-a-type-of-noodle-soup-that-is-widely-popular-in-japan-and-in-ramen-shops-throughout-the-world-although-the-origins-of-ramen-noodles-in-a-soup-broth-are-rooted-in-china-and-chinese-immigrants-that-settled-in-the-country-in-the-late-19-th-and-early-20-th-centuries-it%E2%80%99s-a-distinctly-japanese-dish-today-with-all-its-toppings-flavorings-and-variations">Japanese ramen is a type of noodle soup that&#8217;s widely popular in Japan and in ramen shops throughout the world. Although the origins of ramen (noodles in a soup broth) are rooted in China, and Chinese immigrants that settled in the country in the late 19<sup>th</sup> and early 20<sup>th</sup> centuries, it’s a distinctly Japanese dish today with all its toppings, flavorings, and variations.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">While it’s mainly popular as a fast, inexpensive, convenient meal in Japan, ramen can also be a surprisingly intricate dish with layers of exquisite flavors. </p>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="0-main-components-of-ramen">Main components of ramen</h2>


<ol style="list-style-type:1;--ale-marker-type: 1" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-b6eec5a8 wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles">
<li><div><strong>Broth and flavoring.</strong> A hearty ramen bowl is nothing if not for the flavorful broth or soup base. The ramen broth is usually a combination of chicken or pork stock, plus dashi. Dashi being the soup stock that is rich in umami flavor and is the backbone of the ramen broth. The additional flavoring (or tare) is the seasoning that primarily defines the type of ramen (as mentioned below).</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Noodles.</strong> The ramen noodles are typically made with wheat flour (or a high protein flour that lends it some bite and texture), salt, water, and an alkaline mineral called kansui. These ingredients come together to give the ramen noodles their characteristic chewy texture and color.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Toppings.</strong> This is where variety is the name of the game, but traditional Japanese ramen toppings include sliced meat (chicken in this recipe, but also pork belly), bean sprouts, nori (dried seaweed), scallions, bamboo shoots, and ramen eggs. </div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter"><img decoding="async" width="700" height="1049" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='700'%20height='1049'%20viewBox='0%200%20700%201049'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Close up of the bowl of ramen showing the shoyu ramen broth. " class="wp-image-25950 perfmatters-lazy" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6081-700x1049.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6081.jpg 700w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6081-467x700.jpg 467w" data-sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" /></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="1-most-popular-types-of-ramen">Most popular types of ramen</h2>


<ol style="list-style-type:1;--ale-marker-type: 1" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-b6eec5a8 wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles">
<li><div><strong>Shoyu ramen &ndash;</strong> The most common type of ramen, and is flavored with soy sauce. As a result, this dish features a dark or cloudy broth (like in this chicken ramen recipe). Shoyu ramen is usually topped with a marinated soft boiled egg.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Shio ramen &ndash;</strong> Seasoned with salt, and features a lighter-colored and lighter-bodied broth.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Miso ramen &ndash;</strong> Seasoned with miso paste (fermented soybean paste), and heavy in umami flavor.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Tonkotsu ramen &ndash;</strong> Perhaps the most involved type of ramen broth to make, this ramen features a thick, rich, complex tonkotsu broth made from simmered pork bones.</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<div class="wp-block-group has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-10571125 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained" style="border-left-color:var(--wp--preset--color--primary);border-left-width:4px;background-color:#fff9f9;padding-top:var(--wp--preset--spacing--40);padding-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--40);padding-bottom:var(--wp--preset--spacing--40);padding-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--40)">
<div class="wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-79fb870f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex">
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="2-why-this-recipe-works">Why this recipe works</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full is-resized"><img decoding="async" width="555" height="532" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='555'%20height='532'%20viewBox='0%200%20555%20532'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="heart symbol" class="wp-image-67045 perfmatters-lazy" style="width:50px" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/favorites.png" /></figure>
</div>


<ul style="margin-right:0;margin-left:0;padding-top:0;padding-right:var(--wp--preset--spacing--40);padding-bottom:0;padding-left:var(--wp--preset--spacing--40);--ale-marker-type: number" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-0977f97c wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles advanced-list-marker-type-none">
<li><div>This is a <strong>classic chicken shoyu ramen bowl</strong>, with a delicious soy sauce chicken broth, topped with caramelized soy chicken, a ramen egg, and other flavorful toppings.</div></li>



<li><div>So, it&rsquo;s an incredibly flavorful ramen bowl that tastes like an authentic shoyu ramen. This chicken ramen recipe has been developed to be as <strong>accessible as possible for home cooks, while retaining authentic flavors</strong>.</div></li>



<li><div>You can <strong>easily adapt this ramen bowl</strong> to use ramen ingredients you have at home. And I show you how.</div></li>



<li><div>You can use store-bought stock, but add additional flavor. I&#8217;ll show you <strong>how to add extra umami flavor</strong> to your shoyu broth too!</div></li>



<li><div>This ramen looks amazing with the lacquered chicken served on the side.</div></li>



<li><div>You can make this upgraded chicken ramen version with the chicken cooked separately, OR make it easier by cooking the chicken IN the shoyu broth.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Top it with a classic ramen egg</strong> that completes the authenticity of this shoyu ramen.</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you love classic chicken ramen, that is simple to make, but can still customize to your taste – then this recipe is for you!</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">My <a href="https://www.theflavorbender.com/easy-pork-ramen/">easy pork ramen</a> has been a hit with my readers, but I realized that I&#8217;ve never shared my easy chicken ramen recipe with you guys! So here I’ll fix that with this flavor-packed easy ramen recipe.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I show you how to make a delicious bowl of ramen that tastes like an authentic shoyu ramen bowl, plus I’ll share shortcuts to make this homemade chicken ramen even easier.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <strong>easy version of this recipe can be made in 30 minutes</strong>, and the more elaborate version (with the caramelized chicken) can take about 45 minutes.</p>
</div>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="3-ramen-ingredients">Ramen ingredients</h2>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="4-for-the-shoyu-ramen-broth-ramen-base">For the shoyu ramen broth (ramen base)</h3>


<ul style="--ale-marker-type: number" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-b6eec5a8 wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles advanced-list-marker-type-none">
<li><div>Chicken stock (unsalted)</div></li>



<li><div>Spring onions (scallions)</div></li>



<li><div>Garlic</div></li>



<li><div>Ginger</div></li>



<li><div>Fresh red chili (or dried red chili)</div></li>



<li><div>Light soy sauce</div></li>



<li><div>Mirin</div></li>



<li><div>Fresh shiitake mushrooms (or dried shiitake mushrooms)</div></li>



<li><div>Dried ramen noodles</div></li>
</ul>
</div>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="1797" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='1200'%20height='1797'%20viewBox='0%200%201200%201797'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Easy shoyu ramen broth ingredients on a marbled countertop. " class="wp-image-50896 perfmatters-lazy" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2264-Copy.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2264-Copy.jpg 1200w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2264-Copy-467x700.jpg 467w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2264-Copy-780x1168.jpg 780w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2264-Copy-1025x1536.jpg 1025w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2264-Copy-40x60.jpg 40w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2264-Copy-340x509.jpg 340w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2264-Copy-699x1047.jpg 699w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2264-Copy-150x225.jpg 150w" data-sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" /></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="5-for-the-chicken">For the chicken</h3>


<ul style="--ale-marker-type: number" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-b6eec5a8 wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles advanced-list-marker-type-none">
<li><div>Mirin</div></li>



<li><div>Dark soy sauce</div></li>



<li><div>Light soy sauce</div></li>



<li><div>Cayenne pepper (optional)</div></li>



<li><div>Garlic</div></li>



<li><div>Boneless chicken thighs (skin on or off)</div></li>



<li><div>Brown sugar (for the glaze)</div></li>



<li><div>Dark soy sauce (for the glaze)</div></li>
</ul>
</div>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="1797" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='1200'%20height='1797'%20viewBox='0%200%201200%201797'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Labeled ingredients to make soy marinated chicken for the chicken ramen bowl on a countertop." class="wp-image-50895 perfmatters-lazy" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-Chicken-Copy.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-Chicken-Copy.jpg 1200w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-Chicken-Copy-467x700.jpg 467w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-Chicken-Copy-780x1168.jpg 780w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-Chicken-Copy-1025x1536.jpg 1025w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-Chicken-Copy-40x60.jpg 40w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-Chicken-Copy-340x509.jpg 340w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-Chicken-Copy-699x1047.jpg 699w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-Chicken-Copy-150x225.jpg 150w" data-sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" /></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="6-toppings-optional-use-whatever-you-have-at-hand">Toppings (optional, use whatever you have at hand)</h3>


<ul style="--ale-marker-type: number" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-b6eec5a8 wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles advanced-list-marker-type-none">
<li><div>Asian greens such as bok choy, yu choy (you can also use spinach, water spinach, kale, Swiss chard etc.)</div></li>



<li><div>Sesame oil (for the greens)</div></li>



<li><div>Soy sauce (for the greens)</div></li>



<li><div>Garlic (for the greens)</div></li>



<li><div>Spring onions (scallions)</div></li>



<li><div>Chili oil</div></li>



<li><div>Bean sprouts</div></li>



<li><div>Seaweed</div></li>



<li><div>Radishes</div></li>



<li><div>Mushrooms</div></li>



<li><div><a href="https://www.theflavorbender.com/ramen-eggs-ajitsuke-tamago/">Ramen egg</a> (or regular soft-boiled egg)</div></li>
</ul>
</div>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="1797" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='1200'%20height='1797'%20viewBox='0%200%201200%201797'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Labeled ingredients to make simple steamed greens to serve with ramen." class="wp-image-50901 perfmatters-lazy" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2291.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2291.jpg 1200w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2291-467x700.jpg 467w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2291-780x1168.jpg 780w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2291-1025x1536.jpg 1025w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2291-40x60.jpg 40w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2291-340x509.jpg 340w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2291-699x1047.jpg 699w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2291-150x225.jpg 150w" data-sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Ingredients to make steamed Asian greens</figcaption></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="7-how-to-make-the-best-chicken-ramen">How to make the best chicken ramen</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This is my favorite way to make homemade chicken ramen – Japanese noodles (straight or wavy) in a delicious soy sauce chicken broth (shoyu broth), topped with a jammy egg marinated in a sweet soy marinade (i.e. ramen egg), with flavor-packed caramelized soy chicken and vegetables!</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I prefer using homemade chicken stock for ramen, but I don’t always have homemade chicken stock at hand.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So, I use store-bought in a pinch. To upgrade the store-bought chicken stock, I simmer it with ginger, spring onions, and some chili (because we like it a little spicy).</p>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained"><div style="background-color: #d9edf7;color: #31708f;border-left-color: #31708f" class="ub-styled-box ub-notification-box wp-block-ub-styled-box" id="ub-styled-box-1e8f1b77-c99d-4daf-a9a7-883ba557a434">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph" id="ub-styled-box-notification-content-">If you&#8217;d like to add more umami flavor to your broth, add a few pieces of dried shiitake mushrooms to the stock as well. As the mushrooms reconstitute and soften in the stock, they will impart their earthy umami flavor to the stock. </p>


</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Bring the stock to a boil over medium high heat (or high heat, depending on your stove), with all the aromatics.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Then reduce the heat to a medium and let it simmer to infuse all the flavors. You can increase the amount of stock if you want to cook the noodles in the broth, but I prefer to cook the noodle separately.</p>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="8-why-cook-the-noodles-separately">Why cook the noodles separately?</h3>


<ul style="--ale-marker-type: number" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-b6eec5a8 wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles advanced-list-marker-type-none">
<li><div>You end up with a clear, flavorful chicken broth for your shoyu ramen.</div></li>



<li><div>If you cook the noodles in the broth / stock, the starches release into the stock and make it heavier and cloudy.</div></li>



<li><div>Also, you will end up consuming all that extra starch that you would otherwise wash away.</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>



<figure class="wp-block-gallery aligncenter has-nested-images columns-2 is-cropped wp-block-gallery-6 is-layout-flex wp-block-gallery-is-layout-flex">
<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="700" height="1049" data-id="25954" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='700'%20height='1049'%20viewBox='0%200%20700%201049'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Process of making the chicken ramen broth - Chicken stock in a saucepan with garlic, ginger, spring onions and chili." class="wp-image-25954 perfmatters-lazy" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-5997.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-5997.jpg 700w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-5997-467x700.jpg 467w" data-sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" /></figure>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="700" height="1049" data-id="25961" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='700'%20height='1049'%20viewBox='0%200%20700%201049'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Shiitake mushrooms added to the shoyu ramen broth in a saucepan." class="wp-image-25961 perfmatters-lazy" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6000.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6000.jpg 700w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6000-467x700.jpg 467w" data-sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" /></figure>
</figure>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="9-broth">Broth</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Other flavorings that I add to the shoyu ramen broth include,</p>


<ul style="--ale-marker-type: number" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-b6eec5a8 wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles advanced-list-marker-type-none">
<li><div><strong>Soy sauce &ndash;</strong> This is what makes the ramen broth a shoyu ramen. You can leave out the soy sauce for a shio ramen base. Or add some white miso for a miso ramen base.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Mirin &ndash;</strong> A classic addition to Japanese ramen that really enhances the flavor. It&rsquo;s a sweet rice wine with a slight tang. Supermarkets usually sell &ldquo;Aji-Mirin&rdquo; which isn&rsquo;t really mirin, but it works just as well.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Garlic &ndash;</strong> I always add garlic, but because there&rsquo;s ginger in the recipe, the garlic isn&rsquo;t a necessity. Or you can leave out the ginger, and just add garlic. It&rsquo;s up to you.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Dried shiitake mushrooms</strong> &#8211; Optional, but a fantastic and easy way to add umami flavor without adding MSG (not that there&#8217;s anything wrong with MSG though, because it makes this broth delicious too!)</div></li>



<li><div><strong>MSG</strong> &#8211; MSG gets a bad rap, but it shouldn&#8217;t! Mono sodium glutamate is also present in tomatoes, and tomatoes are incredible delicious! So if you have MSG, you can absolutely add a pinch of this to your broth too. </div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="10-noodles">Noodles</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">What type of noodles can you use for this shoyu ramen bowl?</p>


<ul style="--ale-marker-type: number" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-b6eec5a8 wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles advanced-list-marker-type-none">
<li><div><strong>Straight ramen noodles &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;Usually this type of noodle is used with heavier broths. But I love these noodles, so I use them regularly for homemade ramen.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Wavy ramen noodles &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;These noodles work better for lighter ramen bases, such as this shoyu ramen recipe. If you do have them, you&rsquo;re more than welcome to use it for this ramen recipe.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Instant ramen noodles &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;Perhaps the most popular type of ramen noodles out there. I prefer not to use these, because these noodles overcook so quickly and swell up in the broth and get soggy. And soggy noodles are an absolute no-go for an authentic ramen bowl. But you&nbsp;<em>can</em>&nbsp;use them for this chicken ramen recipe if you like. Just make a note to under-cook them a bit, so that they don&rsquo;t get soggy in the ramen broth.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Udon, soba, somen noodles &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;These are usually sold as dry noodles in supermarkets. You can use these too if you prefer.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Fresh noodles vs dried noodles &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;You can use either! If you have access to fresh ramen noodles, just make sure to cook them according to the instructions on the packet.</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter"><img decoding="async" width="700" height="1049" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='700'%20height='1049'%20viewBox='0%200%20700%201049'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Large white bowl with cooked straight ramen noodles in it." class="wp-image-25948 perfmatters-lazy" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6034-700x1049.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6034.jpg 700w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6034-467x700.jpg 467w" data-sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" /></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="11-chicken">Chicken</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Here’s a pro tip on how to cook the chicken for this chicken ramen (easy chicken version vs classic chicken version).</p>


<ul style="--ale-marker-type: number" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-b6eec5a8 wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles advanced-list-marker-type-none">
<li><div><strong>For the easy version &ndash;&nbsp;</strong>To make this chicken ramen even easier, you can cook the chicken RIGHT IN THE BROTH, and then shred the chicken so that it gets mixed with the ramen noodles and broth. This does save time, but personally,&nbsp;I much prefer to cook the chicken separately, because it adds another great layer of flavor to your ramen bowl.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>For the classic version &ndash;&nbsp;</strong>Make the caramelized soy chicken separately. Making caramelized soy chicken is easy, and you wouldn&rsquo;t believe how delicious the results are! The chicken is marinated in a soy, dark soy, and mirin mix (the same marinade that I use for&nbsp;<a href="https://www.theflavorbender.com/ramen-eggs-ajitsuke-tamago/">ramen eggs</a>), and then grilled in the oven OR on a pan.</div></li>
</ul>
</div>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1200" height="1797" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='1200'%20height='1797'%20viewBox='0%200%201200%201797'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Chicken thighs in a freezer ziploc bag with marinate ingredients added to it." class="wp-image-50898 perfmatters-lazy" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2248.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2248.jpg 1200w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2248-467x700.jpg 467w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2248-780x1168.jpg 780w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2248-1025x1536.jpg 1025w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2248-40x60.jpg 40w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2248-340x509.jpg 340w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2248-699x1047.jpg 699w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Ramen-Ingredients-2248-150x225.jpg 150w" data-sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" /></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I find it easier to do this in the oven,&nbsp;because this way I can cook more chicken at the same time (extras are great for fried rice!), and it leaves the stove free for the ramen broth and noodles.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For both versions of chicken, you can use chicken thighs OR chicken breast pieces. I prefer boneless chicken thighs, because chicken breasts dry out very easily when overcooked. Chicken thighs are more forgiving.</p>
</div>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter"><img decoding="async" width="700" height="1049" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='700'%20height='1049'%20viewBox='0%200%20700%201049'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Two pieces of caramelized soy chicken made with chicken thigh pieces on a white plate." class="wp-image-25957 perfmatters-lazy" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6023-700x1049.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6023.jpg 700w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6023-467x700.jpg 467w" data-sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" /></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h3 class="wp-block-heading" id="12-ramen-toppings">Ramen toppings</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I could never have enough ramen toppings. But there’s only so much that your ramen bowl can hold…</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There are several classic ramen toppings and they all add great flavor to your ramen bowl.</p>


<ul style="--ale-marker-type: number" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-b6eec5a8 wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles advanced-list-marker-type-none">
<li><div><strong>Negi</strong>&nbsp;<strong>or chopped spring onions (green onions) &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;A must for any and every ramen bowl.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Tamago or eggs &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;Another topping that I just have to have. You can serve the eggs however you like; hard boiled, soft boiled, plain, or marinated (<a href="https://www.theflavorbender.com/ramen-eggs-ajitsuke-tamago/">ramen eggs recipe</a>).</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Sesame oil &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;I love adding a drizzle of sesame oil to my shoyu ramen, even if it isn&rsquo;t a classic option. If I&rsquo;m making shio ramen or miso ramen, then I might add a little butter instead.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Sesame seeds &ndash;&nbsp;</strong>You can use white or black sesame seeds (the white stands out against the caramelized chicken, but the black will stand out against the noodles. Ultimately toasted sesame seeds are like little drops of flavor bombs!</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Radishes or sweet bell peppers &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;Another topping that&rsquo;s not traditional, but I like them because they add a nice crunch and sweetness to my ramen bowl.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Mushrooms &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;Shiitake mushrooms are my preferred choice for this shoyu ramen, but I also love enoki. Add a delicious umami flavor, and I also like how meaty they taste.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Steamed or stir-fried greens &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;Baby bok choy (or Shanghai bok choy), bunch spinach, water spinach, gai lan, yu choy are all excellent toppings in a ramen bowl.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Fried chili and garlic, or fried garlic &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;I buy this from the store, because it&rsquo;s easier and we put it on everything. Even steamed rice.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Naruto or Kamaboko &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;Steamed fish cake. I absolutely love this topping when I eat ramen at a restaurant, but I don&rsquo;t add this when making homemade ramen.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Nori or Furikake &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;Sometimes I add this, sometimes I don&rsquo;t. I do love furikake on my&nbsp;<a href="https://www.theflavorbender.com/pork-loco-moco-mazemen-spicy-pork-ramen/">Mazemen bowl</a>&nbsp;though.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Moyashi &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;Raw bean sprouts add some sweetness to the ramen, as well as a nice crunch factor.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Crushed red pepper</strong>&nbsp;&ndash; I personally like to add a few flakes of red chili pepper on top. This isn&rsquo;t traditional, but a personal favorite for me.</div></li>
</ul>
</div>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter"><img decoding="async" width="700" height="1049" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='700'%20height='1049'%20viewBox='0%200%20700%201049'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Ramen toppings including caramelized soy chicken, sauteed Asian greens, sliced green onions, radishes, and ramen eggs." class="wp-image-25956 perfmatters-lazy" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6018-700x1049.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6018.jpg 700w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6018-467x700.jpg 467w" data-sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" /></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="13-ingredient-substitutions">Ingredient substitutions</h2>


<ul style="--ale-marker-type: number" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-b6eec5a8 wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles advanced-list-marker-type-none">
<li><div><strong>Soy sauce &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;If you don&rsquo;t have soy sauce at home, you can leave it out and just season it with salt or white miso. If you&rsquo;re looking for a GLUTEN FREE option, use TAMARI instead.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Dark soy sauce &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;You can use regular soy sauce instead.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Mirin &ndash;</strong>&nbsp;If you don&rsquo;t have mirin, you&nbsp;<em>should</em>&nbsp;get a bottle, because it&rsquo;s so good! And essential for making teriyaki.</div></li>



<li><div>But if you don&rsquo;t have mirin, you can use a dry sherry or dry white wine. Also, you can use a little rice wine with brown sugar tomimic the sweetness. If you want to keep it alcohol free, just omit the mirin, and add about 1/2 tbsp of brown sugar + 1/2 tbsp water, per 1 tbsp of mirin.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>And if you don&rsquo;t have any of the toppings?</strong>&nbsp;You don&rsquo;t really need any fancy toppings for an amazing chicken ramen bowl. Spring onions, a soft-boiled egg, and chicken will do the job just fine too.</div></li>



<li><div>Feel free to add anything else you have at hand that you like to eat for your shoyu ramen. My husband usually adds peas and carrots to this when he takes it to work.</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter"><img decoding="async" width="700" height="1049" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='700'%20height='1049'%20viewBox='0%200%20700%201049'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Easy chicken ramen served in a white bowl with caramelized soy chicken, ramen eggs and other toppings." class="wp-image-25958 perfmatters-lazy" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6060-700x1049.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6060.jpg 700w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6060-467x700.jpg 467w" data-sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" /></figure>



<div class="wp-block-group has-custom-primary-alt-light-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained" style="padding-top:0;padding-bottom:0">
<div class="wp-block-group is-nowrap is-layout-flex wp-container-core-group-is-layout-79fb870f wp-block-group-is-layout-flex">
<figure class="wp-block-image alignleft size-large is-resized"><img decoding="async" width="308" height="312" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='308'%20height='312'%20viewBox='0%200%20308%20312'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt class="wp-image-63981 perfmatters-lazy" style="width:80px" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/icon-embellishment-circle-flask.svg" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="14-my-best-recipe-tips-"><strong>My best recipe tips</strong></h2>
</div>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Here are some pro tips to cut down the cooking time when making this easy chicken ramen. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This homemade chicken ramen recipe takes about 45 minutes because I cook the chicken separately. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I prefer to cook the ramen noodles separately as well. But here are some ways to make this recipe even faster using accessible ramen ingredients, and cut it down to 25 – 30 minutes!</p>


<ul style="--ale-marker-type: number" class="wp-block-list is-layout-flow wp-container-core-list-is-layout-b6eec5a8 wp-block-list-is-layout-flow has-advanced-list-styles advanced-list-marker-type-none">
<li><div><strong>Ready to use, store-bought chicken stock or broth </strong>eliminates one step altogether.</div></li>



<li><div>Rather than adding large pieces of garlic cloves and ginger, <strong>mince or chop them finely in a spice grinder</strong>. That way you don&rsquo;t need to worry about straining them out later.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Add dried shiitake mushroom</strong> to the stock along with the garlic, ginger and spring onions, so that it will add even more umami flavor to the shoyu ramen broth. </div></li>



<li><div><strong>Add the chicken to the stock at the same time as the garlic, ginger, and spring onions</strong>. This will allow the chicken to cook as the stock is infused with all of the flavors. Once the chicken is cooked (about 20 minutes), you can chop or shred it and add it back to the base, along with mushrooms.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Steam the greens in the microwave, or blanch the greens in the ramen broth. </strong>Spinach only takes 1 minute to steam in the microwave. I add a little oil, garlic, and soy sauce and steam in the microwave, and then it&rsquo;s ready to be used.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>Using instant noodles will cut down on cooking time for the ramen noodles.</strong> If I do use instant ramen, I prefer to use NongShim Shin Ramyun ramen packets or Samyang ramen packets. They are thicker and hardy, and stay nice and firm for longer.</div></li>



<li><div>If you&rsquo;re using dried ramen noodles, you can increase the amount of stock (about 1/3 cup per person), and <strong>cook the noodles RIGHT IN THE BROTH too.</strong> Keep in mind though, this will make the broth more cloudy because of the released starches. This is why I prefer cooking the ramen noodles separately.</div></li>



<li><div>If you didn&rsquo;t have time to make the ramen egg earlier, no worries. <strong>It only takes about 10 minutes to make an egg, that&rsquo;s not marinated.</strong> Just boil water, and cook the eggs for 6 &ndash; 6.5 minutes and then they are ready to eat as soon as they are cool enough to peel. You can boil the eggs while the ramen broth is being prepared.</div></li>



<li><div><strong>You can also cook the egg in different ways.</strong> If you don&rsquo;t want to half boil an egg, you can just fry an egg, over easy, and serve it on top of the ramen too!</div></li>



<li><div>You can also use the broth as a base, and <strong>top the ramen with leftover rotisserie chicken (or other leftover meat)</strong>. This really cuts down on the time!</div></li>
</ul>
</div>



<div class="wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So, there you have it. This homemade ramen is nothing if not flavor-packed! I hope you guys love this chicken ramen recipe just as much as we do. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you&#8217;re a fan of noodle recipes, then this chicken ramen is a must try!</p>
</div>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter"><img decoding="async" width="700" height="1049" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%20width='700'%20height='1049'%20viewBox='0%200%20700%201049'%3E%3C/svg%3E" alt="Overhead view of easy chicken ramen, showing ramen noodles, ramen egg, grilled chicken, greens, mushrooms and radishes." class="wp-image-25951 perfmatters-lazy" data-src="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6084-700x1049.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6084.jpg 700w, https://www.theflavorbender.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/01/Easy-Chicken-Ramen-The-Flavor-Bender-6084-467x700.jpg 467w" data-sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" /></figure>



